**`src/api/routes.js`**: the router still calls these functions with a callback. Without this change, `/users` and `/users/:id` would never respond, even though the unit tests would pass. I'd connect the two to the router's callbacks:
```js
{ method: 'GET', path: '/users', handler: (req, cb) => listActiveUsers().then((d) => cb(null, d), cb) },
{ method: 'GET', path: '/users/:id', handler: (req, cb) => findUser(Number(req.params.id)).then((d) => cb(null, d), cb) },
```

If you allow writes, I'll apply these and then run `node --test tests/user-service.test.js` and `node scripts/check-routes.js` to confirm both the tests and the routes work.

